{:edb/db-before-t 1224 :edb/db-after-t 1225 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534537 50 #inst "2026-10-07T21:15:44.486Z" 13194139534537 true] [17592186184004 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^3 -3x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2} - 3 \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x & > 0 \\\\ x & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty)$." 13194139534537 false] [17592186184004 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186184005 1100 "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4$." 13194139534537 false] [17592186184005 1100 "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186184005 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 2x^{3} - x^{4} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 6x^{2} - 4x^{3} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 12x - 12x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12x - 12x^{2} & > 0 \\\\ 12x^{2} - 12x & < 0 \\\\ x(x - 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/b2ab07481181ff8e94caf76114a28445.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1$. So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,1)$.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) < 0$. By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1) > 0$, which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$" 13194139534537 false] [17592186184005 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186184006 1100 "The graph below shows $y=g''(x)$, where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5]$. Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/bbbc1786039bf107ff38f347061b540d.png)" 13194139534537 false] [17592186184006 1100 "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" 13194139534537 true] [17592186184006 1145 "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$.\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x)$, we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5)$. Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5)$." 13194139534537 false] [17592186184006 1145 "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293673 13194139534537 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293680 13194139534537 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293715 13194139534537 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293720 13194139534537 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293726 13194139534537 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293687 13194139534537 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293694 13194139534537 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293701 13194139534537 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293732 13194139534537 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293753 13194139534537 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293708 13194139534537 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293739 13194139534537 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293746 13194139534537 true] [17592186184685 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534537 true] [17592186184685 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139534537 true] [17592186184685 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/q-31214.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y = f^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above.\nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139534537 false] [17592186184685 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,4)$ only. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,4)$ only." 13194139534537 true] [17592186184686 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534537 true] [17592186184686 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/3891159e9d53769e4f95176a2585e339ca347e39aa91c9df8cd6456f8d3155da.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down." 13194139534537 true] [17592186184686 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/q-31229.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y = g^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nFind the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down." 13194139534537 false] [17592186184686 1145 "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293673 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293674 13194139534537 true] [17592186293673 1089 "q-114260" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293673 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534537 true] [17592186293673 1100 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ is concave up." 13194139534537 true] [17592186293673 1113 #uuid "c643932b-c8cc-535d-94b8-66da48e8c389" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293673 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n6x-6>0\\,⟹\\,x>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293675 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293676 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293677 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293678 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293679 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1041 "selection" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293679 13194139534537 true] [17592186293674 1110 #uuid "5cc25a94-cdab-5a5a-9c48-14eeb1210853" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293675 1034 "The function is concave up nowhere." 13194139534537 true] [17592186293675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293675 1111 #uuid "635742ee-51f9-52cc-9da0-9449b0337897" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293676 1034 "(0,1)" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293676 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293676 1111 #uuid "5decf4a0-9685-5b59-9536-adb0c5339bc2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293677 1034 "(-\\infty ,1)" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293677 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293677 1111 #uuid "3d0d60d4-c808-524a-bd39-cfa7eb24e8a4" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293678 1034 "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293678 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293678 1111 #uuid "a4b7b74a-d832-51e5-8564-65f071fc3ccd" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293679 1034 "(1,\\infty )" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293679 1111 #uuid "ffb500c6-43db-5af6-ae0a-64079df0da88" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293680 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293681 13194139534537 true] [17592186293680 1089 "q-114264" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293680 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534537 true] [17592186293680 1100 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={e}^{1-x}+2x$ is concave down." 13194139534537 true] [17592186293680 1113 #uuid "e55fdbac-6bb5-5bac-b529-e0d157ba40d2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293680 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={e}^{1-x}+2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{1-x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-(-{e}^{1-x})={e}^{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ Notice that ${f}^{″}(x)={e}^{1-x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere." 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293682 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293683 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293684 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293685 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293686 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1041 "selection" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293682 13194139534537 true] [17592186293681 1110 #uuid "56d1b0ff-6a07-567b-9fd3-3ca303fca709" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293682 1034 "The function is concave down nowhere." 13194139534537 true] [17592186293682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293682 1111 #uuid "338ad745-d92e-5d74-ae7b-3720d22adfc3" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293683 1034 "(0,\\infty )" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293683 1111 #uuid "c3fbd523-d309-565b-a178-9af0d2d74f92" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293684 1034 "(-\\infty ,0)" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293684 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293684 1111 #uuid "21cf0eeb-d81a-5899-bdd3-d7ed197c9483" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293685 1034 "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293685 1111 #uuid "ae1f8aa3-97c2-546c-ba84-b9400e634203" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293686 1034 "(-\\infty ,-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293686 1111 #uuid "7dbc211b-d700-5c2b-a41c-d50d81c70814" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293687 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293688 13194139534537 true] [17592186293687 1089 "q-114270" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293687 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534537 true] [17592186293687 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293687 1113 #uuid "7af01e6f-dc86-5725-9b0b-63602f01e9af" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293687 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293689 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293690 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293691 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293692 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293693 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1041 "selection" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293693 13194139534537 true] [17592186293688 1110 #uuid "71107757-ea07-51d8-90b3-bf50cdd589cf" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293689 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293689 1111 #uuid "8ffde426-a97f-5dd2-afa0-be7cd1c04dca" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293690 1034 "I only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293690 1111 #uuid "57a153c2-4712-5abd-aeb8-86da041cec06" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293691 1034 "III only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293691 1111 #uuid "03d4f9be-e445-52f2-9e85-2b5fc15af3e6" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293692 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293692 1111 #uuid "66e76bc3-16ac-5dbd-8410-79bf932a02c3" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293693 1034 "II only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293693 1111 #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293694 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293695 13194139534537 true] [17592186293694 1089 "q-114271" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293694 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534537 true] [17592186293694 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293694 1113 #uuid "c7ee43f3-f778-5c3e-9099-6bca7ddcc6dc" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293694 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293696 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293697 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293698 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293699 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293700 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1041 "selection" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293698 13194139534537 true] [17592186293695 1110 #uuid "fc355f65-3bf9-5528-8a74-4acdf8839e82" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293696 1034 "II and III only