7 true] [17592186293712 1034 "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293712 1111 #uuid "020deb07-301a-5991-92aa-3b798d0d7394" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293713 1034 "$(-4,-2)$ only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293713 1111 #uuid "3aa6e347-8860-58c3-a4db-e20bd94b1518" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293714 1034 "$(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(-2,2)$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293714 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293714 1111 #uuid "c44ded78-e720-5260-b84c-0946aea05651" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293715 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293716 13194139534537 true] [17592186293715 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293718 13194139534537 true] [17592186293715 1089 "q-344330" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293715 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534537 true] [17592186293715 1100 "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=-3{e}^{2x}+5$ is concave down.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293715 1113 #uuid "f7f18171-79a5-5247-b3e6-61ea19eed26d" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293715 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=-3{e}^{2x}+5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-6{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-12{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{2x} & <0 \\\\ {e}^{2x} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\,-\\infty ,\\infty \\,).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293716 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293717 13194139534537 true] [17592186293716 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534537 true] [17592186293716 1041 "field-1" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293716 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293717 13194139534537 true] [17592186293716 1110 #uuid "66041a7a-060b-5392-8ff9-f42202ea03d2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293717 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293717 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293717 1111 #uuid "608df557-e420-5090-bc41-1b36aa8b47a4" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293719 13194139534537 true] [17592186293718 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534537 true] [17592186293718 1041 "field-2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293718 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293719 13194139534537 true] [17592186293718 1110 #uuid "972def23-d3a0-5f35-bddb-299a30ef9d34" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293719 1034 "\\infty" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293719 1111 #uuid "5e59b9ca-130b-5234-bfd9-b73e48f251ac" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293720 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293721 13194139534537 true] [17592186293720 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293724 13194139534537 true] [17592186293720 1089 "q-344334" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293720 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534537 true] [17592186293720 1100 "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=6x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)$ is concave down.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293720 1113 #uuid "674e1510-2c32-55cc-bff1-6ee60c46a419" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293720 1145 "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=6x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6+\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x>-1$ we have $(x+1)^{2}<(x+4)^{2},$ and thus $\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}>\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}<\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}$ is always true in the given domain, and we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is $(\\,-1,\\infty \\,).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293722 13194139534537 true] [17592186293721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293723 13194139534537 true] [17592186293721 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534537 true] [17592186293721 1041 "field-1" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293721 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293723 13194139534537 true] [17592186293721 1110 #uuid "0eee1233-c6dc-5118-9310-93fe8b149f48" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293722 1034 "-4" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293722 1111 #uuid "b982f57b-4388-574a-b450-26ddcc0d466a" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293723 1034 "-1" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293723 1111 #uuid "073f8123-cb98-5c22-8816-3e60cde51af0" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293724 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293725 13194139534537 true] [17592186293724 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534537 true] [17592186293724 1041 "field-2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293724 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293725 13194139534537 true] [17592186293724 1110 #uuid "c2397e37-cab1-5a5a-ad83-b194b672069f" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293725 1034 "\\infty" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293725 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293725 1111 #uuid "7f347a15-e82a-53cf-8e47-6e7a629d6456" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293726 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293727 13194139534537 true] [17592186293726 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293730 13194139534537 true] [17592186293726 1089 "q-344340" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293726 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534537 true] [17592186293726 1100 "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4}$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293726 1113 #uuid "37de4824-560d-5bed-91bb-680586ddf82b" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293726 1145 "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\ne -2.$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x)(2x+4)-2{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{(4x+8)(2x+4)^{2}-4(2{x}^{2}+8x)(2x+4)}{(2x+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(x+2)^{2}-8{x}^{2}-32x}{(2x+4)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{2}+32x+32-8{x}^{2}-32x}{(2x+4)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =4(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+2)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ (x+2)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ x+2 & >0 \\\\ x & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\,-2,\\infty \\,).$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293728 13194139534537 true] [17592186293727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293729 13194139534537 true] [17592186293727 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534537 true] [17592186293727 1041 "field-1" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293727 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293729 13194139534537 true] [17592186293727 1110 #uuid "e55671b9-82a2-5326-b519-bc17684c1d68" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293728 1034 "2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293728 1111 #uuid "9d267a85-9025-5d21-b40e-214393ef55e9" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293729 1034 "-2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293729 1111 #uuid "66fa2171-1d8e-5f9a-9333-ce9602127094" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293731 13194139534537 true] [17592186293730 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534537 true] [17592186293730 1041 "field-2" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293730 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293731 13194139534537 true] [17592186293730 1110 #uuid "03da742a-0e06-546a-92c8-9f6f0039a74b" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293731 1034 "\\infty" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534537 true] [17592186293731 1111 #uuid "463a6292-e181-5420-9f32-056cae39a71a" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293732 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293733 13194139534537 true] [17592186293732 1089 "q-78960" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293732 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534537 true] [17592186293732 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293732 1113 #uuid "bf81ab16-a4e9-51ca-976b-3909948534cd" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293732 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $[0,\\pi ].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0$ when $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi .$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ which has the solution $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293734 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293735 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293736 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293737 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293738 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1041 "selection" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293737 13194139534537 true] [17592186293733 1110 #uuid "7c615065-85e9-5941-ae1b-b5ba93c5bde0" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293734 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293734 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293734 1111 #uuid "5c28336b-f7c0-5a09-964d-ad3bdebc6b17" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293735 1034 "I only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293735 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293735 1111 #uuid "590a86a7-7b83-5e23-b212-b72dd4a821bb" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293736 1034 "III only" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293736 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534537 true] [17592186293736 1111 #uuid "ff08702c-faac-5647-937f-5eae3fc72903" 13194139534537 true] [17592186293737 1034 "I